      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-1-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Viral hepatitis is one of the world’s most important public \nhealth challenges. Hepatitis B virus (HBV) and hepatitis \nC virus (HCV) continue to cause chronic infection in hundreds of millions of people, leading to cirrhosis, liver cancer and over 1 million premature deaths each year, even \nthough both infections are preventable and treatable. \nTogether, HBV and HCV account for over 95% of global \nmortality caused by viral hepatitis, with hepatitis A virus \n(HAV), hepatitis D virus (HDV) and hepatitis E virus (HEV) \naccounting for the remainder.\nPeople can be infected with viral hepatitis thro",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The introduction to the Global hepatitis report 2026 explains that hepatitis B and C cause chronic infection in hundreds of millions of people and over 95% of viral hepatitis deaths, and outlines how the five hepatitis viruses are transmitted. It recalls the commitment of all Member States to eliminate viral hepatitis as a public health threat by 2030 and the five interventions that can achieve it, from hepatitis B vaccination to testing and treatment, alongside research on a hepatitis B cure and a hepatitis C vaccine. It describes the report as the fourth global assessment since reporting began in 2016, covering 2015–2024 and drawing on data from 140 reporting Member States and model-based estimates, and sets out the chapter structure. Box 1.1 gives basic facts on each hepatitis virus, and the section ends with an overview of the latest status against the 2030 impact and service coverage targets.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
