    {
      "id": "library/10665-385385/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Global commitments, strategies and targets",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-2-global-commitments-strategies-and-targets.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "and targets \nfor countries to develop national strategies, expand \nvaccination coverage (including a timely birth dose), \nstrengthen infection control, provide comprehensive services for people who inject drugs (e.g. needle and syringe \nprogrammes and opioid agonist therapy) and ensure \nequitable access to affordable diagnostics and medicines. \nPolitical commitment to combating viral hepatitis entered a new phase in 2015, when the UN General \nAssembly adopted the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable \nDevelopment. The Sustainable Development Goals \n(SDGs) included hepatitis within SDG target 3.3, which",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Traces how viral hepatitis rose onto the global health agenda, from a 1975 Health Assembly resolution on blood safety and the 1992 call to add hepatitis B vaccine to infant schedules, through the 2010 and 2014 resolutions, World Hepatitis Day, the Global Hepatitis Programme and the 2015 SDG target 3.3. It describes WHO's first Global health sector strategy on viral hepatitis (2016–2021), which set the goal of elimination by 2030 with a 90% cut in new infections and a 65% cut in deaths from 2015 levels, five intervention areas, a two-phase expansion and a diagnosis and treatment cascade. The section ends with a table of the current 2030 impact and service coverage targets and the latest status of progress against them.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
