    {
      "id": "library/10665-385385/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Epidemic status and trends",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 49,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-3-epidemic-status-and-trends.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "There are five hepatitis viruses: A, B, C, D and E (HAV, \nHBV, HCV, HDV and HEV). This chapter focuses on HBV \nand HCV, because they account for more than 95% of viral \nhepatitis-related deaths (Chapter  1) and are the forms \nof hepatitis for which global targets have been set in the \nWorld Health Organization (WHO) Global health sector \nstrategy on viral hepatitis, 2022–2030 (GHSS 2022–2030) \n(Chapter 2). \nPeople can be infected with HBV and HCV through \nexposure to infected blood (HBV and HCV) or other bodily \nfluids (HBV). In highly endemic areas, most chronic HBV \ninfections occur in child",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opens the chapter on the status and trends of the viral hepatitis epidemic, which concentrates on hepatitis B and C because they cause over 95% of hepatitis deaths, explaining how each virus is transmitted, how infection progresses silently over decades to cirrhosis and liver cancer, and how incidence, prevalence and mortality are defined. Reviews the available interventions (hepatitis B vaccination, antiviral treatment and prophylaxis, a 12-week direct-acting antiviral cure for hepatitis C, blood safety, infection control and harm reduction) and the 2030 elimination targets. Its key findings put 2024 deaths at about 1.1 million from HBV and 240 000 from HCV, with HBV deaths up 17% since 2015 and concentrated in the African and Western Pacific regions, HCV deaths down 12%, and an estimated 240 million people living with chronic HBV and 47 million with HCV infection. Closes with a section on hepatitis D, which weighs most on low- and middle-income countries with limited testing, and a table comparing the latest coverage indicators with their 2030 targets.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
