        "text": "Presents the latest coverage data for hepatitis prevention, diagnosis and treatment, recalling that HBV and HCV caused 1.3 million deaths and 1.8 million new infections in 2024 and setting out the coverage levels the global health sector strategy requires by 2025 and 2030. Covers the most impactful interventions first: hepatitis B birth-dose and infant vaccination (with 118 of 194 Member States providing universal birth-dose vaccination as of December 2025), antiviral prophylaxis against mother-to-child transmission, and antiviral treatment for chronic HBV and HCV infection. Later sections address blood and injection safety in health care and harm-reduction services for people who inject drugs, who account for about 44% of new HCV infections. Concludes that harm-reduction coverage is far below recommended levels, with about 35 needles and syringes distributed per person who injects drugs each year against a 2030 target of 300, only 18 in 100 having access to OAMT, and only five countries reaching high coverage of both services.",
