      "target": "../sections/sec-006-5-the-road-to-elimination.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The possibility that viral hepatitis could be eliminated \nas a public health threat worldwide was first recognized \nby all Member States of the World Health Organization \n(WHO) in 2014 (Chapter 2). Subsequently, the goal of the \nfirst WHO global health sector strategy on viral hepatitis (GHSS 2016–2021) (4), adopted by all Member States \nin 2016, was defined as elimination of viral hepatitis as a \npublic health threat by 2030. This goal was retained in the \nsecond GHSS, for 2022–2030 (3).\nThe 2030 elimination targets are: \n\t\nf a 95% reduction in the annual number of new hepatitis \nB virus (HBV",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Traces the goal of eliminating viral hepatitis as a public health threat by 2030 from its recognition by WHO Member States in 2014 through the two global health sector strategies, and notes that the targets can be met with existing vaccines and antivirals whose prices have fallen sharply since 2015. Finds progress between 2015 and 2024 too slow: new HBV infections fell by 32% and new HCV infections by 8.1%, HBV-related deaths rose by 17% while HCV-related deaths fell by 12%, and in 2024 birth-dose coverage was 45%, under 5% of people with HBV were on treatment and HCV treatment coverage was 20%. Sets out five priorities for global and regional action: scaling up HBV treatment, especially in the African and Western Pacific regions, and HCV treatment, especially in the Eastern Mediterranean Region; raising birth-dose vaccination and antiviral prophylaxis coverage, especially in Africa; and making nonmedical injections safer through harm reduction. Gives 10 country examples, ending with Thailand's free nationwide HCV test-and-treat campaign, which in three years reached 7.3 million people, found almost 75 000 infections and treated more than 50 000.",
